---
import GuideLayout from "@/layouts/GuideLayout.astro";
import { GUIDES_BY_SLUG } from "@/data/guides";
const g = GUIDES_BY_SLUG["california-overtime-rules"]!;
---

<GuideLayout title={g.title} metaTitle={g.metaTitle} description={g.description} slug={g.slug} updated={g.updated}>
  <p>
    California is the strictest overtime state in the US. Non-exempt employees
    earn overtime by the <strong>day</strong> as well as by the week:
    time and a half after 8 hours in a workday, double time after 12, and
    special rates on the seventh consecutive day of a workweek. Federal law
    only looks at the week, so a California paycheck can include overtime even
    in a week of fewer than 40 hours.
  </p>

  <h2>The rules in one table</h2>
  <p>California Labor Code §510 sets these minimums for non-exempt employees:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Hours worked</th><th>Minimum rate</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Up to 8 in a workday (and up to 40 in the workweek)</td><td>Regular rate</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Over 8 and up to 12 in a workday</td><td>1.5&times; regular rate</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Over 12 in a workday</td><td>2&times; regular rate</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Over 40 in the workweek (hours not already paid as daily overtime)</td><td>1.5&times; regular rate</td></tr>
      <tr><td>First 8 hours on the 7th consecutive workday of the workweek</td><td>1.5&times; regular rate</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Over 8 hours on that 7th consecutive day</td><td>2&times; regular rate</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    "Regular rate" here means the same thing it does under federal law: your
    hourly wage plus most other pay for the week, such as shift differentials
    and non-discretionary bonuses. See
    <a href="/guides/how-to-calculate-overtime-pay/">how to calculate overtime pay</a>
    for what goes into it.
  </p>

  <h2>What counts as a "workday"</h2>
  <p>
    The daily thresholds apply to a <em>workday</em>, which the California wage
    orders define as any consecutive 24-hour period beginning at the same time
    each calendar day. It does not have to start at midnight. If your
    employer's workday runs from 6:00 AM to 6:00 AM, a shift from 10:00 PM to
    7:00 AM is split across two workdays: 8 hours fall in the first workday and
    1 hour in the next. That matters for night workers, and it is covered in
    more depth in <a href="/guides/overnight-shift-hours/">how to calculate
    hours for overnight shifts</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <em>workweek</em> is a fixed, regularly recurring period of seven
    consecutive workdays. Your employer chooses when it starts, but it can't be
    moved around to avoid overtime.
  </p>

  <h2>Daily and weekly overtime are not added together</h2>
  <p>
    California counts overtime in two passes, and an hour is never paid as
    overtime twice:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Daily pass.</strong> For each workday, hours 8 to 12 are paid at
      1.5&times; and hours beyond 12 at 2&times;.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Weekly pass.</strong> Add up the hours that were <em>not</em>
      already paid as daily overtime. If that total is over 40, the excess is
      paid at 1.5&times;.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Daily overtime hours are left out of the weekly count so they aren't
    "pyramided" into a second premium. This is the order the
    <a href="/calculators/time-card-calculator/">Time Card Calculator</a> uses
    when you choose the California rule.
  </p>

  <h2>Worked example: a 50-hour week</h2>
  <p>
    A warehouse employee earns $24.00 an hour and works six days in one
    workweek:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>Hours</th><th>Regular</th><th>1.5&times;</th><th>2&times;</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Monday</td><td>10</td><td>8</td><td>2</td><td>0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tuesday</td><td>9</td><td>8</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Wednesday</td><td>8</td><td>8</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Thursday</td><td>13</td><td>8</td><td>4</td><td>1</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Friday</td><td>6</td><td>6</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Saturday</td><td>4</td><td>4</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Daily totals</strong></td><td><strong>50</strong></td><td><strong>42</strong></td><td><strong>7</strong></td><td><strong>1</strong></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    After the daily pass there are 42 regular hours. That is 2 more than 40,
    so the weekly pass moves 2 of them (the last ones worked, on Saturday)
    to 1.5&times;. The final split is <strong>40 regular, 9 overtime and 1
    double-time hour</strong>:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Regular: 40 &times; $24.00 = $960.00</li>
    <li>Overtime: 9 &times; $36.00 = $324.00</li>
    <li>Double time: 1 &times; $48.00 = $48.00</li>
    <li><strong>Gross pay: $1,332.00</strong></li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Under the federal rule alone the same 50 hours would be 40 regular and 10
    overtime hours, or $1,320.00. The $12 difference is the one hour past 12
    on Thursday being paid at double time instead of time and a half. In a
    week with several long days and fewer total hours, the gap is much bigger:
    four 11-hour days (44 hours) earn 12 daily overtime hours in California
    but only 4 under the federal rule.
  </p>

  <h2>The seventh-day rule</h2>
  <p>
    If you work all seven days of a workweek, the seventh day is treated
    differently: its first 8 hours are paid at 1.5&times; even if you haven't
    reached 40 hours, and any hours beyond 8 on that day are paid at 2&times;.
    For example, someone who works 5 hours on each of the first six days (30
    hours) and 9 hours on the seventh is owed 8 hours at 1.5&times; and 1 hour
    at 2&times; for that last day.
  </p>
  <p>
    The rule depends on the <em>workweek</em>, not any seven days in a row.
    Seven consecutive days that straddle two workweeks don't trigger it. Our
    calculators apply California's daily and weekly thresholds but
    <strong>not</strong> the seventh-day rule, so if it applies to you, add
    those hours separately.
  </p>

  <h2>Alternative workweek schedules</h2>
  <p>
    An employer can adopt an "alternative workweek schedule", such as four
    10-hour days, if it follows the secret-ballot election procedure in the
    wage orders. Under a valid schedule, hours up to the agreed daily length
    (10 hours in a 4/10 schedule) are paid at the regular rate, and daily
    overtime starts after that. Without a valid election, a 4/10 schedule
    owes 2 hours of overtime every day. If you work a compressed schedule, ask
    whether it was adopted by an election.
  </p>

  <h2>Who isn't covered</h2>
  <p>
    Overtime rules apply to <strong>non-exempt</strong> employees. Most
    executive, administrative and professional employees are exempt if they
    pass a duties test <em>and</em> earn a salary of at least twice the state
    minimum wage for full-time work. With the 2026 statewide minimum wage of
    $16.90 an hour, that threshold is $70,304 a year. Some jobs, such as
    certain computer professionals, physicians and outside salespeople, have
    their own rules. Job titles don't decide exemption; the duties and pay do.
    See <a href="/guides/exempt-vs-non-exempt-employees/">exempt vs non-exempt
    employees</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes on California timesheets</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Only checking the weekly total.</strong> A 38-hour week can still
      include overtime if any day ran past 8 hours.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Counting daily overtime twice.</strong> The weekly pass uses only
      the hours not already paid as daily overtime.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Using the base wage for the overtime rate</strong> when a shift
      differential or production bonus was earned that week. Both raise the
      regular rate.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Forgetting that a workday may not start at midnight.</strong>
      Night-shift hours are assigned to the workday your employer defines.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Calculate a California week</h2>
  <p>
    Enter each day's start and end times in the
    <a href="/calculators/time-card-calculator/">Time Card Calculator</a> and
    choose the California rule to get the daily and weekly split and your
    gross pay. For a quick check from total hours, the
    <a href="/calculators/overtime-calculator/">Overtime Calculator</a> also has
    a California option. Double time is explained further in
    <a href="/guides/what-is-double-time-pay/">what is double-time pay?</a>
  </p>

